1:[["$","a",null,{"className":"skip-link","href":"#main","children":"Skip to content"}],["$","header",null,{"className":"site-header","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"header-inner","children":["$","$L6",null,{"page":"fractals"}]}]}],["$","main",null,{"className":"fractals-page","id":"main","children":["$","section",null,{"className":"fractals-hero shell","children":[["$","$L7",null,{"className":"page-back","href":"/","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"←"}],"Back to main page"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"fractals-page-intro","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"A short introduction"}],["$","h1",null,{"children":"What are fractals?"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"There is no single definition that captures every object mathematicians call a fractal. People often describe fractals as shapes whose detail continues to appear at smaller and smaller scales."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"fractals-primer","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Many fractals are self-similar: they are made from smaller, scaled copies of themselves. Repeating a simple rule can produce intricate geometry. Many fractals have a non-integer dimension, although not every fractal is self-similar or has a non-integer dimension."}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"fractals-dimension","aria-labelledby":"dimension-heading","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"fractals-dimension-copy","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"A quick idea of dimension"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"dimension-heading","children":"What does dimension measure?"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"fractals-dimension-explanation","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Dimension is one way of describing how the amount of detail in a shape changes with scale. For an exactly self-similar shape, the similarity dimension compares the number of smaller copies with their size relative to the whole."}],["$","p",null,{"children":["If the whole consists of ",["$","var",null,{"children":"N"}]," smaller copies of itself, each with linear scale factor 1/",["$","var",null,{"children":"r"}],", its similarity dimension ",["$","var",null,{"children":"d"}]," satisfies ",["$","var",null,{"children":"N"}]," = "," ",["$","var",null,{"children":"r"}],["$","sup",null,{"children":["$","var",null,{"children":"d"}]}],". Equivalently, ",["$","var",null,{"children":"d"}]," = log(",["$","var",null,{"children":"N"}],") / log(",["$","var",null,{"children":"r"}],")."]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"dimension-figures","children":[["$","figure",null,{"className":"dimension-figure","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"dimension-picture dimension-line","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","span",null,{}],["$","span",null,{}]]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Line · dimension 1"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"2 copies, each with linear scale factor 1/2."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"dimension-equation","children":"d = log 2 / log 2 = 1"}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"dimension-figure","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"dimension-picture dimension-square","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","span",null,{}],["$","span",null,{}],["$","span",null,{}],["$","span",null,{}]]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Square · dimension 2"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"4 copies, each with linear scale factor 1/2."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"dimension-equation","children":"d = log 4 / log 2 = 2"}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"dimension-figure","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"dimension-picture dimension-gasket","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","$L8",null,{}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Sierpiński gasket · dimension ≈ 1.585"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"3 copies, each with linear scale factor 1/2."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"dimension-equation","children":"d = log 3 / log 2 ≈ 1.585"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"dimension-closing","children":["The gasket's dimension lies between 1 and 2. Although it is more intricate than a line, its two-dimensional area is exactly zero: after ",["$","var",null,{"children":"n"}]," construction steps, the remaining area is (3/4)",["$","sup",null,{"children":"$L9"}]," times the area of the original triangle, and this tends to zero as ","$La"," increases. Its two-dimensional "," ","$Lb"," ","is therefore zero. "," ","$Lc"," ","generalises length and area to arbitrary dimensions, including non-integer ones. At the gasket's dimension, ","$Ld"," = log(3) / log(2), its ","$Le","-dimensional Hausdorff measure is finite and positive."]}]]}],"$Lf","$L10","$L11"]}]}],"$L12"]
